Q: What is a TCP handshake? (simple)

Handshake = **agree to talk before sending real data**.
Like calling someone:

1) You: “Can you hear me?”
2) They: “Yes, I can hear you — can you hear me?”
3) You: “Yes. Let’s talk.”

Only after that you start the real conversation (HTTP request, etc.).

TCP is **connection-oriented**, so client and server first set up a connection with a **3-way handshake**.

────────────────────────────────────────
Why needed?

Before HTTP/API data:
- Both sides confirm they are reachable
- Agree on starting sequence numbers (for ordered reliable delivery)
- Connection moves to ESTABLISHED

Without this, TCP wouldn’t know the other side is ready.

────────────────────────────────────────
The 3 steps (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK)

Imagine:
Client = your browser / Postman
Server = Spring Boot on port 8080

Step 1 — SYN (client → server)
Client: “I want to connect” (SYNchronize)
Flags: SYN
Meaning: please start a TCP connection

Step 2 — SYN-ACK (server → client)
Server: “OK, I got your request, and I also want to sync”
Flags: SYN + ACK
Meaning: I acknowledge you, and here’s my sync too

Step 3 — ACK (client → server)
Client: “OK, acknowledged”
Flags: ACK
Meaning: connection ready

Now connection = **ESTABLISHED**
Then application data starts (HTTP GET/POST, TLS, etc.)

Diagram:

Client                         Server
  |                              |
  | -------- SYN --------------> |
  |                              |
  | <----- SYN + ACK ----------- |
  |                              |
  | -------- ACK --------------> |
  |                              |
  | ===== connection ready ===== |
  | -------- HTTP request -----> |
  | <------- HTTP response ----- |

────────────────────────────────────────
Tiny real-life analogy

Phone call:
1) You dial (SYN)
2) They pick up “Hello?” (SYN-ACK)
3) You say “Hi, it’s Afham” (ACK)
4) Then you discuss the topic (HTTP data)

UDP has **no** handshake — it just sends (like throwing a letter).
Details: `How UDP works explained simply`

────────────────────────────────────────
What is ACK?

ACK = acknowledgment = “I received your packet.”
TCP uses ACKs later too, for every data segment, so lost data can be resent.
Handshake is just the **first** use of SYN/ACK to open the connection.

────────────────────────────────────────
Closing later (don’t confuse)

Opening = 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK)
Closing ≈ FIN/ACK exchange (connection teardown)

Interview usually asks about **opening** handshake.

────────────────────────────────────────
TCP handshake vs TLS handshake (don’t mix)

1) TCP handshake first → pipe is open
2) If HTTPS → TLS handshake next → encrypt the pipe
3) Then HTTP request/response

Order:
DNS → TCP handshake → (TLS handshake if HTTPS) → HTTP

TLS handshake is different: certificates, keys, cipher agreement.
TCP handshake is only: “can we open a reliable connection?”

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring Boot view

When Postman calls `http://localhost:8080/users`:
1) TCP 3-way handshake to port 8080
2) HTTP request bytes sent on that connection
3) Tomcat accepts connection, Spring handles request

If handshake fails (firewall/port closed) → connection refused / timeout
(your controller never runs)

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“TCP 3-way handshake is SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK — client and server agree to establish a connection before sending application data like HTTP.”
